## Chapter 1 Physics

Spontaneous Breaking of Chiral SymmetryIn the quantum field theory, spontaneously broken symmetry plays a major role in the applications of QCD and also find applications in the theory of the strong interactions. For the chiral symmetry, because the chiral partners, _e.g._, the \(\rho\) and \(a_{1}\) mesons have a mass difference of \(\simeq\) 550 MeV/\(c^{2}\)[1], this observation of the mass splitting indicates a spontaneous breaking of chiral symmetry. It is because the vacuum expectation value of the quark condensate \(\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\neq 0\). In consequence of this non-vanishing value, the vector current \(j_{V}=j_{L}+j_{R}\) is still conserved while the axial vector symmetry (\(j_{A}=j_{L}-j_{R}\)) is spontaneously broken. The axial-vector charge \({\cal Q}_{i}^{A}\) in Eq. 1.7 is still commutes with the QCD Hamiltonian, but the ground state \({\cal Q}_{0}^{A}|0\rangle\neq 0\). According to the Goldstone theorem [10], the chiral symmetry is spontaneously broken when the ground state is chosen, and results in the existence of eight massless Goldstone bosons (\(\pi^{\pm},\pi^{0},K^{\pm},K^{0},\overline{K^{0}},and\ \eta\)).

For a simple example, consider a scalar self-interacting real field Lagrangian,

\[{\cal L}=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{\mu}\phi\partial^{\mu}\phi-V(\phi), \tag{1.17}\]

with following potential

\[V(\phi)=\frac{1}{2}\mu^{2}\phi^{2}+\frac{1}{4}\lambda\phi^{4}. \tag{1.18}\]

The Lagrangian is invariant under the transformation

\[\phi\rightarrow-\phi. \tag{1.19}\]

The vacuum (\(\phi_{0}\)) can be obtained from the Hamiltonian

\[{\cal H}=\frac{1}{2}[(\partial_{0}\phi)^{2}+(\triangledown\phi)^{2}]+V(\phi). \tag{1.20}\]

We can see that \(\phi_{0}=constant\) corresponds to the ground state of \(V(\phi)\) where

\[\phi_{0}(\mu^{2}+\lambda\phi_{0}^{2})=0. \tag{1.21}\]To ensure the \({\cal H}\) is bounded, the \(\lambda\) should be positive. The sign of \(\mu\) determined the minimum of the \(V(\phi)\), for \(\mu^{2}>0\), there is just one vacuum at \(\phi_{0}=0\) and it is invariant under Eq. 19 (see Fig. 3(a)). However, for \(\mu^{2}<0\), the state \(\phi_{0}=0\) is a local maximum and therefore unstable. The ground state become two vacua states \(\phi_{0}^{\pm}=\pm\sqrt{-\mu^{2}/\lambda}\) (Fig. 3(b)). The choice of one ground state between \(\phi_{0}^{+}\) or \(\phi_{0}^{-}\) does not effect the invariance of the Lagrangian under transformation Eq. 19. Nevertheless, once one choice is made the symmetry is spontaneously broken since \({\cal L}\) is invariant but vacuum is not. If a continuous symmetry is spontaneously broken, with the identification of the fields \(\phi^{*}\phi\) around the symmetry axis, _i.e._, axial-vector rotations, correspond to the massless Goldstone bosons. In contract, radial excitations correspond to massive particles. Therefore it contains one massless scalar particle for each broken generator of the original symmetry group.

As a further consequence of chiral symmetry breaking, the axial current matrix element between the vacuum and a Goldstone boson is nonvanishing. The finite quark masses lead to a contribution for the quark condensate

\[\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\equiv\langle 0|\overline{\psi}_{L}\psi_{R}+ \overline{\psi}_{R}\psi_{L}|0\rangle, \tag{22}\]

in the Lagrangian of Eq. 1, which exhibits the explicit mixing of left- and right-handed quarks in the QCD vacuum. For pions, one has the vacuum expectation value of such Goldstone bosons which is:

\[\langle 0|j^{\mu}_{A,k}(x)|\pi_{j}(p)\rangle=i\delta_{jk}f_{\pi}p^{\mu}e^{-ipx}. \tag{1.23}\]

The pion decay constant \(f_{\pi}=93\) MeV is an order parameters which measures the strength of the symmetry breaking. The vacuum expectation value or quark condensate Eq. 1.22 is a second order parameter. The order parameters \(f_{\pi}\) and \(\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\) are related. They can be expressed as the Gell-Mann-Oakes-Renner relation (GOR) [12]

\[m_{\pi}^{2}f_{\pi}^{2}=-2\overline{m}\langle\overline{q}q\rangle, \tag{1.24}\]

where \(\overline{m}=\frac{1}{2}(m_{u}+m_{d})\) and \(\langle\overline{q}q\rangle=\langle\overline{u}u\rangle=\langle\overline{d}d\rangle\). Taking \(\overline{m}=6\) MeV yields the vacuum value for the quark condensate, \(\langle\overline{q}q\rangle=-(240~{}MeV)^{3}=-1.8~{}fm^{-3}\).

In-Medium Condensates and Chiral RestorationIn the ultra-relativistic heavy-ion collisions, it is to be expected that the quark and gluon condensates are modified in a hot and dense medium created in the collisions. From the schematic view of the QCD phase diagram Fig. 1.2. It is expected that hadronic matter go through a phase transition at high temperature and pressure. The quarks condensates are melted in the medium, and before reaching the deconfinement stage, chiral symmetry is partially restored.

To discuss the modifications, we start from a given system of 3-volume V with finite temperature, \(T\) and quark chemical potential, \(\mu_{q}\) in contact with a heat bath which is specified by the grand canonical partition function

\[{\cal Z}(V,T,\mu_{q})=Tr\{e^{-(\hat{H}-\mu_{q}\hat{N})/T}\}, \tag{1.25}\]

where \(\hat{H}\) is the Hamiltonian of the system and \(\hat{N}\) is the quark number operator.

The expectation value of the quark condensates can be given by

\[\langle\langle\bar{\psi}\psi\rangle\rangle={\cal Z}^{-1}\sum_{n}\langle n| \bar{\psi}\psi|n\rangle e^{-(E_{n}-\mu_{q})/T}, \tag{1.26}\]where the sum extends over a complete set of eigenstates of \(H\) and _E\({}_{n}\)_. The \(|n\rangle\) and _E\({}_{n}\)_ are the QCD eigenstates and energies, respectively. Considering a non-interacting pion gas, the leading order result to the vacuum condensate \(\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\) is therefore given by the matrix element \(\langle\pi|\overline{\psi}\psi|\pi\rangle\), and the leading order result to Eq. 1.26 with respect to the vacuum expectation value ratio becomes

\[\frac{\langle\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\rangle}{\langle\overline{\psi} \psi\rangle}\simeq 1-\frac{\sum_{\pi}\rho_{\pi}^{s}(T)}{f_{\pi}^{2}m_{\pi}^{2}} \tag{1.27}\]

where \(\sum_{\pi}\) denotes the pion \(\sigma\)-term and \(\rho_{\pi}^{s}\) the pion scalar density at given temperature. For the case of finite baryon density and vanishing temperature, the result is a linear decrease with density

\[\frac{\langle\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\rangle}{\langle\overline{\psi} \psi\rangle}\simeq 1-\frac{\sum_{N}\rho_{N}^{s}(\mu_{N})}{f_{\pi}^{2}m_{\pi}^{2}} \tag{1.28}\]

where \(\sum_{N}\) is the nucleon \(\sigma\)-term and \(\rho_{N}^{s}\) the nucleon scalar density at given \(\mu_{q}\) (\(\mu_{N}=3\mu_{q}\)). At nuclear saturation densities (\(\rho_{0}=0.16fm^{-3}\)) the quark condensate has dropped by 30% with a experimentally determined nucleon \(\sigma\)-term of \(\sigma_{N}\approx 45\) MeV [11]. Based on these calculations, the expected scalar quark condensate as function of temperature and density can be derived, which is displayed in Fig. 1.4.

Chiral meson Lagrangians should be scale invariant on the classical level for dimension 4. From the derivation of Brown and Rho [12], the effective Lagrangian has appropriate scaling behavior of the QCD Lagragian. They predict that the quark and gluon condensates change in dense matter, and the meson masses will decrease when the quark condensates are proposed. The change in the quark condensate at a given density can be expressed as

\[\frac{\langle\langle\overline{\psi}\psi\rangle\rangle}{\langle\overline{\psi} \psi\rangle}=(\frac{\chi^{*}}{\tilde{\chi}_{0}})^{3}, \tag{1.29}\]

where \(\tilde{\chi}_{0}\) denotes the vacuum expectation value of the \(\chi^{*}\) field. So an in-medium pion decay constant can be defined as

\[f_{\pi}^{*}=f_{\pi}\frac{\chi^{*}}{\tilde{\chi}_{0}}. \tag{1.30}\]

As a result of the derivation [12], a universal Brown-Rho (BR) scaling relation of the in-medium vector meson masses can be expressed as

\[\Phi(\rho)\equiv\frac{f_{\pi}^{*}}{f_{\pi}}=\frac{m_{\sigma}^{*}}{m_{\sigma}}= \frac{m_{N}^{*}}{m_{N}}=\frac{m_{\rho}^{*}}{m_{\rho}}=\frac{m_{\omega}^{*}}{m_{ \omega}}. \tag{1.31}\]

The typical values for \(\Phi(\rho_{0})=0.82\pm 0.06\) at normal nuclear matter density, indicates a dropping of the \(\rho\) mass by 15-20%. More details of other relative models of medium modification of vector mesons can be found in Ref. [RW00].

### 1.2 Di-leptons

In the ultra-relativistic heavy ion collisions, the study of di-lepton was one of probes to map out the behavior of the hot and dense matter created in the collision. Being produced at all stages of the collision and suffering minimal final-state interaction, di-leptons convey a variety of information about the high density and high temperature matter and become a unique tool to study the properties of QGP.

Fig. 1.5 shows the schematic di-lepton mass spectrum in ultra-relativistic heavy ion col

Figure 1.4: The expectation value of the quark condensate. Calculation is based on the Nambu-Jona-Lasinio model [KLW90]. Figure is from [Her01].

lisions includes the major sources, they consist of Drell-Yan process, hadron gas/resonanses, heavy flavor hadron contribution, and QGP thermal radiation _etc._. The contributions of those sources dedicate to different di-lepton invariant mass region due to their production mechanism.

In the high mass region (\(m_{ll}>3\) GeV/\(c^{2}\)), di-leptons stem from hard processes (mostly Drell-Yan annihilation and heavy quarkonia). Drell-Yan process is the annihilation of a quark in one nucleus with an antiquark of another nucleus, creating a virtual photon or Z boson which then converts into a pair of leptons. This process is occurring in the early pre-equilibrium stage of the collision, it can be described by a Feynman diagram shown in Fig. 1.6. The heavy quarkonium contributions are from \(J/\psi\) and \(\psi^{\prime}\). Comparing to the \(J/\psi\), the charmed hadron contribution are small in this mass region. But at intermediate mass region, it is a dominant source.

In the intermediate mass region (\(1.1<m_{ll}<3\) GeV/\(c^{2}\)), the di-leptons are dominated by the semi-leptonic decays of charm, bottom hadrons and the QGP thermal radiations. Charm and bottom quarks are produced in the inelastic hard scattering processes between

Figure 1.5: Schematic di-lepton mass distribution of ultra-relativistic heavy ion collisions.Figure is from [10].

two constituent quarks (\(q\overline{q}\to g^{*}\to Q\overline{Q}\), here \(Q\overline{Q}=c\bar{c}\) or \(b\bar{b}\), respectively). Then the open charm and bottom mesons are formed out of a heavy flavor quark (\(c\) or \(b\)) and a light quark (\(u,d\) or \(s\)). After a weak decay process, they produce single leptons, _e.g._, \(D^{+}\to\bar{K^{0}}l^{+}\nu_{l}\). Therefore, the decays of both \(D^{+}\) and \(D^{-}\) form a di-lepton pair. If there is no enhancement of open-charm production, a thermal signal from the QGP is expected to be revealed in this mass region [25, 26]. The thermal di-lepton is considered to bring the information of temperature and radial flow of the equilibrium thermalized partonic matter.

The low mass region is dominated by the soft process involving the light quark sector. The di-lepton continuum come from Dalitz decays of neutral mesons such as \(\pi^{0},\eta,\eta^{\prime}\to\gamma l^{+}l^{-}\) and \(\omega\to\pi^{0}l^{+}l^{-}\). The two body decays of resonance, for example \(\rho,\omega,\phi\to l^{+}l^{-}\), compose the peaks. This region is particularly sensitive to the in-medium modification of vector mesons which is possible relative to the chiral symmetry restoration. The di-lepton production is mediated by the \(\rho\) meson which has broad width (149 MeV [27]) in the hadronic matter. Due to the short life time of only 1.3 \(fm/c\) and strong coupling to the \(\pi\pi\) channel, the di-lepton signals from \(\rho\) undergo a significant modification in medium. The signature can directly be interpreted as the space-time averaged spectral function. QGP thermal radiation in this mass range are not significant. For detail theoretical calculations of thermal di-lepton at SPS and RHIC energies see, _e.g._, Ref. [28].

Figure 1.6: Feynman diagram for Drell-Yan process.

### 1.3 Exotic decay

Rare processes like leptonic decays of hadrons provide possible observables in searching for traces of new physics beyond the Standard Model (BSM) [10, 11, 12, 13]. These decays usually involve electromagnetic or weak couplings which can be calculated to high accuracy. In addition to a direct observation of the Higgs boson, the Large Hadron Collider (LHC) looks to explore BSM physics. Precision measurements at low energy target deviations of observables from the SM prediction as indirect evidence of coupling from BSM physics to the rare process [10]. The pseudoscalar mesons (for example, \(\eta\) or \(\eta^{\prime}\) ) are particularly interesting since their decay to \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs is suppressed by \(\alpha^{2}(10^{-4})\) and helicity conservation due to the small electron mass (\(r^{2}=(m_{e}/m_{P})^{2}\simeq 10^{-6}\)). The branching ratio (B.R.) of \(\eta\to e^{+}e^{-}\) can be predicted in SM with good precision, which is \(2.3\times 10^{-9}\). Couplings from BSM physics can increase this B.R. significantly [10]. RHIC provides a unique opportunity to search for BSM physics with rare decays of hadrons. The high luminosity and high multiplicity in nucleus-nucleus collisions produce copious hadrons of interest for the rare decay process. With recent upgrades to the detector [14], including the data acquisition system [15], the STAR Collaboration is able to benefit from high rate capability as well as excellent lepton identification at low momentum in the search for rare decays from the copiously produced hadrons.

### 1.4 Previous experimental results

There are several measurements on the di-lepton continuum in heavy ion collisions at a variety of energies. The most relevant measurements to this analysis are the measurement of the dimuon continuum by the NA60 collaboration at SPS and the di-electron continuum measurement from PHENIX at RHIC. In this section, the results from two experiments will be briefly discussed.

#### Na60

The NA60 experiment has been proposed to study the deconfined partonic matter at SPS heavy ion collisions. The detector implements the muon spectrometer and zero degree calorimeter used in NA50 [1] with two state-of-the-art silicon detectors. Benefit from the new detectors, NA60 has the ability to identify the distance between collision vertex and the muon tracks. The dimuon contribution from charmed hadron can be distinguished from the prompt \(\mu^{+}\mu^{-}\) pairs [14].

One of their results, the centrality-integrated net dimuon mass spectrum for 158A GeV In-In collisions in the LMR region is shown in the left panel of Fig. 1.7. All sources of hadron decays except \(\rho\) decays are included, excess dimuons are isolated from the known sources. The excess dimuons can be illustrated by the modified \(\rho\) contributions, which is shown in the right panel of Fig. 1.7. Two main theoretical scenarios for the in-medium spectral properties of the \(\rho\). An in-medium broadened spectral function [15] and the dropping mass scenario [1], were used for the comparison. The broadening describes the data well.

Another result is mass spectrum in the IMR region, which is shown in Fig. 1.8. NA60

